45.response:
Let \(\beta\) denote the axis perpendicular to \(\alpha\). For a 2D parabolic band \(b=c,v\), the directional transport weight is

\[
A_{b\alpha}\propto \frac{\sqrt{m_{bx}m_{by}}}{m_{b\alpha}}
=\sqrt{\frac{m_{b\beta}}{m_{b\alpha}}}.
\]

In the nondegenerate intrinsic limit, the electron and hole Seebeck coefficients have equal magnitude and opposite sign, while the common activation factor \(e^{-\Delta/(2k_BT)}\) cancels from the sign of the total Seebeck coefficient. Since \(\tau_c=\tau_v\),

\[
S_\alpha\propto
\frac{A_{v\alpha}-A_{c\alpha}}
     {A_{v\alpha}+A_{c\alpha}}.
\]

Goniopolarity requires \(S_xS_y<0\), so electron and hole conduction must dominate along opposite axes. Defining the 2D density-of-states masses

\[
m_c=\sqrt{m_{cx}m_{cy}},\qquad
m_v=\sqrt{m_{vx}m_{vy}},
\]

the condition is

\[
\boxed{
\left(
\frac{m_{v,x}}{m_{c,x}}
-\sqrt{\frac{m_{v,x}m_{v,y}}{m_{c,x}m_{c,y}}}
\right)
\left(
\frac{m_{v,y}}{m_{c,y}}
-\sqrt{\frac{m_{v,x}m_{v,y}}{m_{c,x}m_{c,y}}}
\right)<0
\;\Longleftrightarrow\;
\frac{m_{c,x}}{m_{c,y}}\neq\frac{m_{v,x}}{m_{v,y}}
}
\]

Thus the conduction- and valence-band anisotropy ratios must differ. If \(m_{v,x}/m_{c,x}>m_{v,y}/m_{c,y}\), the \(x\)-direction is n-type and the \(y\)-direction is p-type; reversing the inequality reverses the polarities.

